  "solution": {
    "approach": "One solution would be to fix top left and bottom right corner and then counting the number of ones. This solution takes O((N*M)^3) as there are (N*M)^2 pairs of cells and for each pair of cells, we asymptotically iterate over matrix. This approach will time out.\n\nSince we only care about square size, Let’s try all square sizes from 1 to min(N, M). For square size S, we need to check if there’s some sub-grid with at most K ones. For this, let’s iterate over all candidates of top-left cell, and count the number of ones in square of side S with current cell as the top-left corner. For all S having atleast one such square, the maximum is the required answer.\n\nUsing prefix sums, the time complexity of this approach is O(min(N, M)*N*M)",
    "timeComplexity": "O(N)",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1)",
